  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B021",
        "laureates": [
          "Michael Berry",
          "Yakir Aharonov"
        ],
        "citation": "for revealing the observable physical significance of quantum phase"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B006",
    "B043"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Aharonov–Bohm and Berry phases established that quantum phase is not merely a calculational convention but contains observable global information. The effects have been tested across many distinct platforms and became organizing concepts in quantum optics, mesoscopic physics, atomic physics, and condensed matter. Berry and Aharonov are unusually clear originators of the two central contributions, and the result has the maturity, conceptual depth, and experimentally grounded reach appropriate for recognition.",
    "case_against": "The ballot combines historically distinct phase phenomena. A citation must therefore state their common physical lesson without implying that they were a single discovery, and we should examine whether prior recognition of related topological ideas makes the boundary insufficiently distinct.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that Katori's lattice-clock concept and Ye's experimental realization have produced exceptionally reproducible atomic control and precision.",
        "response": "I agree that B043 is a highly mature and cleanly attributable alternative, and it remains my strongest experimental alternative. I nevertheless give B021 priority because it changed the physical content assigned to quantum phase itself; the clocks are an extraordinary realization of precision control, whereas the phase discoveries supply a more fundamental and broadly unifying insight."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig notes that Aharonov–Bohm and Berry phases are distinct enough that their grouping needs a compelling conceptual formulation, and also cautions that related topological concepts have already received recognition.",
        "response": "This is the central qualification. I would avoid a broad claim about all topology and instead confine the citation to the observable significance of quantum phase. Both contributions demonstrate how global phase structure produces measurable consequences; that narrow formulation preserves their common insight while respecting their separate histories."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that dynamical mean-field theory transformed correlated-electron physics through a quantitative and broadly usable theoretical framework.",
        "response": "B020 is a major intellectual achievement, but its formulation and subsequent development are more distributed, and its prize boundary as a method is less sharp. B021 has a clearer pair of foundational discoveries and unusually direct experimental confirmation."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the two phase effects can be joined under a sufficiently precise common citation without obscuring their distinct histories.",
      "Whether earlier prizes for related topological concepts require a still narrower delineation of the novelty of B021."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
